Praxis depends on the following software that is not a plain upstream crate.
Each entry says where it comes from, its license, and why Praxis carries it.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Pingora (fork)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Crates:  quixotic-plecostomus-cache, quixotic-plecostomus-core,
         quixotic-plecostomus-error, quixotic-plecostomus-header-serde,
         quixotic-plecostomus-http, quixotic-plecostomus-lru,
         quixotic-plecostomus-pool, quixotic-plecostomus-proxy,
         quixotic-plecostomus-runtime, quixotic-plecostomus-rustls,
         quixotic-plecostomus-timeout
Source:  https://github.com/praxis-proxy/pingora, a fork of Cloudflare
         Pingora (https://github.com/cloudflare/pingora) maintained by the
         Praxis project
License: Apache License, Version 2.0
Why:     Upstream Pingora does not expose all of the TLS customization hooks
         and proxy body handling that Praxis needs. The fork's functional
         changes from upstream are listed in FORK.md in the fork's
         repository. The fork is intended to be temporary.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
rustls-openssl (vendored)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Crate:   quixotic-plecostomus-rustls-openssl (library name rustls_openssl)
Source:  a vendored copy of rustls-openssl 0.4.1
         (https://github.com/tofay/rustls-openssl) with FIPS
         provider-routing fixes, published from the Pingora fork above
License: MIT
Why:     Praxis installs it at startup as its rustls cryptography provider,
         so TLS cryptography runs in the system OpenSSL. This is what lets
         the FIPS build use the OpenSSL FIPS provider.

The rustls-openssl license follows.

